menu "Station: web server"

    config WEB_PBKDF2_ITERATIONS
        int "Admin password hash cost (PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 iterations)"
        range 1000 1000000
        default 3000
        help
            Iterations for a newly set admin password. Measured on the
            ESP32-S3: about 1.0 s per 10 000 iterations, so 3000 take about
            0.3 s (PROVISIONAL until measured again on the station). The
            check runs on the web server's task, which answers nothing else
            meanwhile; the login throttle bounds how often one client can
            make it run. The count is stored with each hash: existing
            passwords keep theirs (10 000 for those set by earlier builds)
            until they are changed, and a login never re-hashes a password
            silently.

    config WEB_REQUEST_DEADLINE_S
        int "Time a client gets to send one request (s)"
        range 5 30
        default 6
        help
            One server task reads every request. From the first byte of a
            request (request line and headers, a JSON body, a WebSocket
            frame) the whole of it must arrive within this time, or the
            connection is closed (408 while headers are read). Without this
            limit a client sending a byte every few seconds could hold the
            server, and every status push queued behind it, for hours. A
            browser sends a request at once, so even a slow phone on the
            setup access point needs a fraction of a second; 6 s also covers
            two lost and resent TCP segments. Uploads are read by the worker
            task at the client's pace and are not bounded by it. Must be
            longer than the 4 s a single receive may wait.

endmenu
